  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Visual Autoregressive Modeling (VAR): Next-Scale Prediction Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "In `code/main.py` you will:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "1. Build a tiny **multi-scale VQ tokenizer** on synthetic \"image\" data (2D Gaussian rings)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "2. Train a **VAR-style transformer** to next-scale-predict the tokens."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "3. Sample by calling the transformer 4 times (4 scales) and decoding."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "4. Verify that scale-ordered training makes generation parallel within a scale."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This is a toy implementation. The point is to see the scale-structured attention mask and the parallel-within-scale generation actually working."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Scale count ablation.** Train VAR with 4, 6, 8, 10 scales. Measure reconstruction quality vs number of autoregressive passes. More scales = finer residuals = better quality but more passes.\n\n2. **Codebook size.** Train tokenizers with codebook sizes 512, 4096, 16384. Larger codebooks give better reconstruction but harder prediction. Find the knee.\n\n3. **Parallel-within-scale check.** For a trained VAR, measure the attention pattern explicitly. Within scale k, does the model attend to cross-scale positions but not intra-scale? Verify the mask implementation.\n\n4. **VAR vs DiT scaling.** For the same ImageNet class-conditional task, train VAR and DiT at matched param budgets (e.g., 33M, 130M, 458M). Plot FID vs compute. VAR should pull ahead of DiT at each size \u2014 reproduce the paper's result at small scale.\n\n5. **Text conditioning.** Extend VAR to take a text embedding (CLIP pooled) as an extra conditioning input via adaLN. This is the HART recipe. How much does FID improve on text-aligned sampling?"
      ]
    }
  ],
